"Based on the provided context, there are several transition-related themes in the conversations:\n\n## Career Transitions Between Specialties\n\n- **Emergency Medicine to Family Medicine**: An EM attending with 10 years of experience is planning to transition to Primary Care Sports Medicine fellowship and eventually clinic work, expressing concerns about adapting from ED procedures to chronic disease management and learning to be comfortable with \"waiting\" for results rather than immediate diagnosis [1jiveh7]\n\n- **Cross-Specialty Fellowship Pathways**: Discussion of addiction medicine as a fellowship option that's accessible to multiple specialties, with family medicine providing particular advantages since \"addiction knows no boundaries\" and FM physicians can manage addiction across all age groups [18co348]\n\n## Practice Model Transitions\n\n- **Corporate to Direct Primary Care (DPC)**: Multiple physicians are transitioning from traditional corporate primary care to DPC models due to burnout from administrative burden, with one physician noting that attending a DPC summit made them believe \"there are some physicians who are going to turn in their letter of resignation when they get back\" [1m8zolg]\n\n- **Hospital-Owned to Independent Practice**: A physician resigned from a hospital-owned PCP network to start a DPC practice, seeking advice on how to word patient notification letters about the transition while noting that hospital leadership \"don't understand\" the DPC model of lower volume [16uf183]\n\n## Training and Career Path Transitions\n\n- **Accelerated Medical Education**: Discussion of a three-year MD program that automatically places students into the school's FM residency, representing a streamlined transition from medical school to residency training [18125hi]\n\n- **Fellowship Training Transitions**: Detailed discussion of addiction medicine fellowship pathways, noting that the field is transitioning to require fellowship training rather than just taking board exams, with the change affecting competitiveness and career planning [18co348]\n\n## Workplace Transitions and Adaptations\n\n- **Managing Patient Expectations During Transitions**: Physicians discuss setting new boundaries when taking over from retiring doctors who had different practice styles, with one noting they \"made it clear with patients that my standards were going to be different\" when transitioning to a new practice model [1mrgzy4]\n\n- **Contract and Employment Transitions**: Discussion of non-compete restrictions and contract negotiations, with physicians seeking part-time arrangements or fighting for reduced patient-facing hours as they transition their work-life balance [1i1ns95]"